## Evolute of the Ellipse — Total Length

**Parametrize the ellipse.** Let
$$x = a\cos t,\qquad y = b\sin t,\qquad 0\le t < 2\pi,$$
and assume $a > b > 0$ (the case $a<b$ is symmetric; $a=b$ gives a circle whose evolute is a point, length $0$).

**Center of curvature.** For a parametric curve, the evolute $(X,Y)$ is
$$X = x - \frac{y'(x'^2+y'^2)}{D},\qquad Y = y + \frac{x'(x'^2+y'^2)}{D},\qquad D = x'y''-x''y'.$$

Compute the derivatives:
$$x'=-a\sin t,\; x''=-a\cos t,\; y'=b\cos t,\; y''=-b\sin t,$$
$$x'^2+y'^2 = a^2\sin^2 t + b^2\cos^2 t,\qquad D = ab\sin^2 t + ab\cos^2 t = ab.$$

Hence
$$X = a\cos t - \frac{b\cos t\,(a^2\sin^2 t+b^2\cos^2 t)}{ab}
     = \frac{\cos t}{a}\bigl[a^2 - a^2\sin^2 t - b^2\cos^2 t\bigr]
     = \frac{\cos t}{a}\,(a^2-b^2)\cos^2 t.$$

Setting $c^2 = a^2-b^2$:
$$\boxed{X = \frac{c^2}{a}\cos^3 t.}$$

Similarly,
$$Y = b\sin t - \frac{a\sin t\,(a^2\sin^2 t+b^2\cos^2 t)}{ab}
   = \frac{\sin t}{b}\bigl[b^2 - a^2\sin^2 t - b^2\cos^2 t\bigr]
   = -\frac{c^2}{b}\sin^3 t.$$

Up to reflection (the curve is symmetric in all four quadrants), the evolute is the **astroid**
$$X = \frac{c^2}{a}\cos^3 t,\qquad Y = \frac{c^2}{b}\sin^3 t,$$
which satisfies $(aX)^{2/3}+(bY)^{2/3} = c^{4/3} = (a^2-b^2)^{2/3}$.

**Arc length.** By the four-fold symmetry,
$$L = 4\int_0^{\pi/2}\!\sqrt{\dot X^2+\dot Y^2}\,dt,$$
with
$$\dot X = -\frac{3c^2}{a}\cos^2 t\,\sin t,\qquad \dot Y = \frac{3c^2}{b}\sin^2 t\,\cos t.$$

Therefore
$$\dot X^2+\dot Y^2 = 9c^4\cos^2 t\,\sin^2 t\!\left(\frac{\cos^2 t}{a^2}+\frac{\sin^2 t}{b^2}\right),$$
and on $[0,\pi/2]$ both $\cos t,\sin t\ge 0$, so
$$L = 4\int_0^{\pi/2} 3c^2\cos t\,\sin t\,\sqrt{\frac{\cos^2 t}{a^2}+\frac{\sin^2 t}{b^2}}\;dt.$$

**Substitution.** Let
$$u = \frac{\cos^2 t}{a^2}+\frac{\sin^2 t}{b^2},\qquad
du = 2\sin t\cos t\!\left(\frac{1}{b^2}-\frac{1}{a^2}\right)dt
    = \frac{2c^2}{a^2b^2}\,\sin t\cos t\,dt.$$

Bounds: $t=0\Rightarrow u=1/a^2$; $\;t=\pi/2\Rightarrow u=1/b^2$ (and $1/a^2<1/b^2$ since $a>b$). Thus $\sin t\cos t\,dt = \dfrac{a^2b^2}{2c^2}\,du$, giving
$$L = 4\cdot 3c^2\cdot\frac{a^2b^2}{2c^2}\int_{1/a^2}^{1/b^2}\!u^{1/2}\,du
  = 6a^2b^2\cdot\frac{2}{3}\Bigl[u^{3/2}\Bigr]_{1/a^2}^{1/b^2}.$$

**Evaluate:**
$$L = 4a^2b^2\!\left(\frac{1}{b^3}-\frac{1}{a^3}\right)
  = 4a^2b^2\cdot\frac{a^3-b^3}{a^3b^3}
  = \frac{4(a^3-b^3)}{ab}.$$

**Sanity check.** When $a=b$ (circle), $a^3-b^3=0$, so $L=0$: the evolute collapses to the center. ✓

---

### Result

The entire length of the evolute of the ellipse $\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1$ (with $a\ge b$) is

$$\boxed{\,L \;=\; \dfrac{4\,(a^3-b^3)}{a\,b}\,}.$$

